# Ghostfolio stack secrets (services VM) — app + Postgres
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   sops -e -i .sops.env
# (encryption needs only the public key in .sops.yaml, so no sudo and no age
# key are required to create this file — only editing one afterwards is.)
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/ghostfolio.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys — the
# stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# Reminder from CLAUDE.md: any value containing '$' must be single-quoted in
# the plaintext before encrypting — the decrypt-and-source path expands it
# otherwise. None of the generators below emit '$', so if you follow them you
# will not hit it.
#
# ⚠ ONE OF THESE MUST NEVER BE ROTATED once accounts exist. See
# ACCESS_TOKEN_SALT.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: REDIS_PASSWORD. Upstream's compose sets one
# and passes it to `redis-server --requirepass` on the command line; this stack
# runs an unauthenticated Redis on the compose project's own private bridge
# network instead, exactly like paperless, wger and dawarich do. The reasoning
# — argv is host-readable, and nothing outside the project can reach the cache
# — is written out in compose.yaml next to the ghostfolio_cache service. Adding
# the variable here WITHOUT also giving redis a `requirepass` breaks the app:
# redis answers AUTH with "ERR Client sent AUTH, but no password is set".

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACCESS_TOKEN_SALT — ⚠ NEVER ROTATE.
#
# Ghostfolio accounts have no password. The ONLY credential is a "security
# token" (a UUID) shown once at sign-up, and the server stores nothing but a
# SHA-256 of `<token><ACCESS_TOKEN_SALT>`. Change this value and every existing
# token stops matching its stored hash — that is not a re-login, it is every
# account on the instance locked out permanently, with no reset flow to
# recover through, because the token IS the identity.
#
# So: generate it once, before the first sign-up, and leave it alone.
#
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ACCESS_TOKEN_SALT=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JWT_SECRET_KEY — signs the session JWTs the security token is exchanged for.
#
# Safe to rotate, unlike the salt above: the only cost is that every logged-in
# browser is signed out and has to present its security token again. Rotate it
# if you ever think a session cookie leaked.
#
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
JWT_SECRET_KEY=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL. ONE key, TWO readers: it initialises the `ghostfolio` role in the
# database container, and compose interpolates it into the app's DATABASE_URL
# (`${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-}` in compose.yaml). There is deliberately no second
# spelling of it to keep in sync.
#
# ⚠ Rotating this is a TWO-STEP change, not an edit: POSTGRES_PASSWORD only
# initialises the role on an EMPTY datadir. On an existing database you must
# also `ALTER ROLE ghostfolio WITH PASSWORD ...` inside the container, or the
# app comes back up unable to authenticate while Postgres itself is perfectly
# healthy.
#
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24
